{
  "requirement": "Each public SDK item has one defining module path. The SDK exposes no public\nre-exports. The chapter that introduces each supported type or free function owns its declaration.\nThe [public API inventory](sdk/public-api.md) links each item to that requirement,\nwhich describes its purpose\nand declares its public Rust interface, including fields, enum variants, trait\nmembers, and inherent methods. A change to that interface requires a change to\nthe owning requirement. Every supported struct with public fields is\n`#[non_exhaustive]`. Its constructor accepts the values needed to create it.\nAdditional fields have consuming builder methods that accept a concrete value.\nOptional fields start as `None`. Backend inference options start empty and can\nbe added one key at a time.\n\nThe SDK’s public modules follow the interfaces introduced in these chapters.\n\n- `domain`: application data and the `Domain` and `NodeContents` traits.\n- `graph`: criterion definitions, graph observations, evidence, and functions for running criteria.\n- `request`: `ValidationContext`, typed `Ask` builders, context values, and request phases.\n- `agent`: configuration for the supplied agents.\n- `sources`: project inspection, search queries, and their results.\n- `outline`: selectable source contents and language rules; `outline::languages`\n  supplies bundled languages, and `outline::selector` defines selector syntax.\n- `storage`: filesystem evidence storage.\n\nSource access, search engines, redaction, provider transports, agent-result paging,\nand conversation machinery are implementation details of those interfaces.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the reviewed tests adequately check `requirements.sdk.public_api`. I found no gaps.\n\n**Obligations and their tests**\n\n1. **One defining module path, and no public re-exports.**\n   - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[sdk_has_no_public_reexports]` parses every `.rs` file under `src` with tree-sitter. It fails on any `use_declaration` whose visibility modifier is exactly `pub`. `pub(crate)` uses are allowed, as intended.\n   - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[public_exports_match_the_appendix]` builds the rustdoc for the crate. `documented_exports` walks the module tables from the crate root and asserts that each `(path, kind)` is unique (\"Duplicate rustdoc export\"). It then compares the full set with the inventory in `md/requirements/sdk/public-api.md`, and reports both missing and unexpected items.\n\n2. **Each item's owning chapter declares its interface, and a change to the interface requires a change to the requirement.**\n   - `documented_exports` calls `check_declaration` for every non-module export. A missing inventory link fails the test.\n   - `check_declaration` loads the owning requirement and requires a prose contract plus a `rust,ignore` declaration. It compares trait impls and member names with rustdoc. It also compares the signature parts (struct and enum declaration, fields, variants, trait members, inherent methods) with rustdoc, reporting differences in both directions.\n   - `tests/public_api.rs#fn[member_order_is_free_but_field_and_method_types_are_committed]` shows that the comparison ignores member order. It also shows that changes to a field type, a return type, or `&mut self` versus `&self` are detected.\n\n3. **Every supported struct with public fields is `#[non_exhaustive]`.** `check_declaration` asserts that a published `pub struct` page with `structfield.` members contains `#[non_exhaustive]`. The signature comparison also covers the attribute, because the spec declarations carry it.\n\n4. **Constructor, builders, optional fields starting as `None`, and options.**\n   - The constructor signatures are enforced through the declaration comparison.\n   - Behavior is checked in `src/agent/config.rs#mod[tests]/fn[backend_builders_set_only_the_requested_fields]`.\n     - The `BedrockConfig`, `OpenAiConfig` and `OllamaConfig` constructors produce only the required fields, and optional fields and `options` are absent when serialized.\n     - Consuming builders set `aws_profile`, `region`, `credentials`, `session_token`, `model`, `endpoint`, `api_key_env` and `api_key`.\n     - `option` adds one key per call. A repeated key is overwritten, and `options` starts empty.\n   - `tests/outline_content.rs#fn[outline_constructors_preserve_fields_and_replace_attachments]` checks the `OutlineItem` optional spans, `name_span` and `attachments`. It also checks `Outline.diagnostic` and the `with_*` builders.\n   - `src/outline/selector.rs#mod[tests]/fn[parses_named_anonymous_and_ordinal_segments]` checks that `Segment::new` leaves `ordinal` as `None` and that `ordinal(n)` sets it.\n   - In `tests/source_defaults.rs`:\n     - `scoped_text_results_keep_line_numbers_context_and_completeness` checks the `TextQuery` defaults and the `scope`, `context_lines` and `limit` builders.\n     - `built_in_languages_and_structural_search_use_the_project_scope` checks the `StructuralQuery` default limit and its `limit` and `scope` builders.\n     - `source_bytes_error_kinds_and_language_names_round_trip` checks `SourceFile::new` leaving `language` as `None` and the `language` builder.\n   - `Record::new` takes `version` as a constructor argument. It is not an additional optional field, so no default test is owed.\n\n5. **Public modules follow the listed chapters.** The module tree is compared with the inventory by the exports test. That test fails if a module is added or removed, or if the contents of a module differ from the inventory.\n\n**Not required**\n- The statement that implementation details are not public is covered by the exact-match export comparison.\n- The `ValidationContext::context(self, value: impl Context)` builder is not a struct with public fields, so the \"concrete value\" rule does not apply to it.\n\nNo `{fixme}` acknowledgments are present or needed.",
  "references": "83e5a024def69d2c249a2488b0d6e064785b414d91c24616a0f007d173d67065",
